    { "answer": ["Alcohol withdrawal","Delirium tremens","DT","DTs"], "clues": ["In 'The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn', Huck's father suffers from this condition where he thought snakes were biting his legs. If that's not helpful, Nicolas Cage also played a character who  in the 1995 film, Leaving Las Vegas. His performance won him the Academy Award for Best Actor in 1996","For centuries, this was misidentified as 'phrenitis' or an inflammation of the brain. It was formally identified and named in 1813, which marked a turning point in the clinical recognition of this as a disease that could be managed medically","Patients might develop confusion, shaking, and vivid hallucinations which popularized an older idiom 'seeing pink elephants'"] }

{ "answer": ["vibrio vulnificus","vibrio","v ulnificus","vulnificus"], "clues": ["Genetic and fossil evidence suggests this bacteria has existed in marine environments for hundreds of millions of years, hitching rides on microscopic zooplankton like copepods.","Hippocrates meticulously documented this disease in a man living on an island in the Aegean Sea. The patient developed black bullous skin lesions and suffered from rapidly progressive septicemia and died on the second day of the illness","Unlike it's more famous cousin that causes cholera, this is primarily known for causing wound infections, particularly in those with hemochromatosis."] },
{ "answer": ["EKG","ECG","Electrocardiogram"], "clues": ["To demonstrate this technology for the first time, British physiologist Augustus Waller used his Samoyed mix named Jimmy. Jimmy was trained to stand patiently with his paws dipped in jars of saline solution.","The first of this machine was built in 1901 and weighed 600 pounds. Kind of like Jimmy, patients had to place both hands and one foot into large buckets of saltwater. Machines now only weigh about 8 pounds","Early versions were labeled with ABCD. Einthoven developed a mathematical formula that compensated for the inertia and friction of the mercury column in the capillary tube and the mathematically corrected reading was subsequently labeled with PQRST."] },
{ "answer": ["Fidaxomicin","dificid"], "clues": ["This drug was initially isolated by Abbott Laboratories in the 1970-80s but they decided to out-license it to PathoGenesis in the 90s. Chiron Corp then acquired PathoGenesis in 2000 but wasn't interested in this medication and sold the licensing rights to a startup, Optimer Pharmaceuticals. Just as Optimer gained FDA approval in 2011, a major compliance scandal emerged and the company imploded. Optimer was bought by Cubist which was then bought by Merck and finally reached the market.","When Abbott Laboratories was developing the drug in the 1980s, the entire pharmaceutical industry was obsessed with finding a cure for MRSA. This drug was fantastic at killing MRSA in test tubes but animal tesing showed that the drug was not absorbed when swallowed.","Because it is barely absorbed, it acts locally in the GI tract and has a narrow spectrum of action. It binds to bacterial RNA polymerase and halts transcription but also uniquely inhibits spore production and toxin formation, lowering the chance of recurrent infection."] },
{ "answer": ["Sciatica"], "clues": ["Hippocrates coined the name of this condition from the Greek word ischios, meaning hip. He thought that symptoms were caused by localized disease or subluxation of the hip joint","The Italian anatomist, Domenico Contugno, proved that this condition is an affliction of the nerve rather than the hip joint. For generations, the condition was widely called Cotugno’s disease. Cotugno also famously discovered cerebrospinal fluid","In 1864, Ernest Lasègue linked low back trouble with leg pain and described the physical mechanics of lifting a straight leg to test for this condition."] },
{ "answer": ["mumps"], "clues": ["During WWI, only influenza and gonorrhea caused more hospitalizations among US troops compared with this disease","In the 1960s, a 5 year old, Jeryl Lynn came down with this disease. Her father rushed to her bedside and cultured the causal virus from her throat. The attenuated virus strain he created is still the primary component used in vaccines worldwide today","The first recorded clinical description of this disease was by Hippocrates where he described large, soft swellings near the ears on one or both sides of the face. Some male patients also suffered from painful swelling of the testicles and many patients experienced a dry cough and hoarseness."] },
{ "answer": ["Polio","poliovirus","poliomyelitis"], "clues": ["In the 1930s, white medical leaders claimed Black people were immune to this disease, leaving many Black children undiagnosed or denied treatment. In 1941, Tuskegee opened the only facility in the US specifically built to treat Black patients with this condition and helped train the next generation of Black orthopedic specialists","Egyptian artwork and skeletal remains show classic signs of limb deformities from this disease dating back over 3,300 years","A 1954 evaluation of a vaccine for this disease involved 1.4 million schoolchildren and was one of the first applications of the double-blind trial method. Salk famously chose not to seek a patent or personal profit to ensure it could be distributed globally as fast as possible saying 'there is no patent. Could you patent the sun?'"] },
{ "answer": ["mycophenolate","MMF","Cellcept","mycophenolate mofetil","mycophenolic acid"], "clues": ["This was first isolated in 1893 from a fungus, Penicillium brevicompactum. When culturing a fungus growing on bad corn, Bartolomeo Gosio noted that the fungus produced this active antibacterial compound. Unfortunately, this discovery was published in an obscure Italian medical journal and went unnoticed until much later and is not used as an antibiotic today","In the late 1980s, a South African geneticist was studying children with rare, severe immunodeficiencies. He noticed that when children lacked a specific enzyme to make guanosine, their T and B cells completely failed to multiply. He realized that this drug intentially mimics this exact defect.","This was approved by the FDA in 1995 and is increasingly utilized off label as a steroid sparing treatment in autoimmune diseases although was originally approved for the prevention of organ transplant rejection"] },
{ "answer": ["Losartan","cozaar"], "clues": ["This was discovered by an inexperienced pharmaceutical team in the early 1980s. The breakthrough moment actually came when an inexperienced team member accidentally gave a rat a massive dose of a compound they were testing. His team member shared 'it was like adding the animal to the drug instead of adding the drug to the animal', but this accident helped them continue work developing this drug","Once this drug is swallowed, the liver converts a good portion to an active metabolite called EXP3174. This metabolite is 10 to 40 times more potent at blocking AT1 receptors than the parent drug, relaxing arterial and venous smooth muscle and dilating efferent arterioles in the kidneys more than afferent.","It is now in the top 10 most commonly prescribed medications in the United States and valued at 1.5 billion dollars globally. Unlike a similar medication, this medication does not affect bradykinin and does not cause patients to cough"] },
{ "answer": ["Histoplasmosis","histoplasma","Histoplasma capsulatum"], "clues": ["In 1905, this was discovered by Dr. Darling during an autopsy where he noticed small intracellular bodies inside cells. He named it 'tissue-lover with a capsule' and for years, the condition was known as 'Darling's disease'","In 1997, Bob Dylan was hospitalized with severe chest pain and doctors discovered he was suffering from pericarditis caused by this dimorphic fungus.","Skin-test surveys revealed that up to 80% of adults living in the Mississippi basin had calcified lung scars, meaning they had encountered the fungus and built natural immunity without ever realizing it was a serious disease. It has been frequently linked to chicken coops, bat caves, bird roosts, and old storm cellars where spores accumulate in the soil"] },
{ "answer": ["Blastomycosis","Blastomyces"], "clues": ["When analyzing the skeletal remains of the Lake Woodland indigenous peoples of North America, who lived roughly between 500 and 1000AD, paleopathologists noticed a stark uptick in destructive bone lesions compared to ealier eras. Researchers hypothesize that as these ancient societies shifted toward intensive agriculture, they inadvertently dug up and inhaled massive amounts of spores from this organism, contributing to shortened lifespans observed during the agricultural transition","Dogs, especially hunting, sporting, and large breeds that spend time sniffing and digging in wet soil, are up to ten times more susceptible to this fungus than humans. It is not typically contageous from animals to humans","In the chilly environment of outdoor dirt and wood, this grows as a harmless, fuzzy mold. However, once its tiny spores are inhaled into the warm human or animal body, they morph into large, multiplying yeasts that can invade tissue. Under the microscope, the yeast can be easily recognized by its unique 'broad-based buds'."] },
